q('A patient with mild-to-moderate Alzheimer dementia asks about established symptomatic cognitive therapy. Which class is appropriate to consider?', 'An acetylcholinesterase inhibitor such as donepezil, galantamine or rivastigmine',[
 ('An anticholinergic such as oxybutynin','Central anticholinergic effects can worsen cognition.'),('A dopamine antagonist as routine memory treatment','This is not a standard cognitive-enhancing strategy.'),('Memantine as mandatory first-line treatment for every mild case','Memantine has a different usual severity/indication profile.')],
 'Cholinergic therapy offers symptomatic benefit', '**Acetylcholinesterase inhibition can modestly improve or stabilize symptoms in selected patients.**', ['It does not restore lost neurons or guarantee arrested progression.','Review gastrointestinal effects, weight, pulse and interactions.'], '**Discuss observable treatment goals.**', ['Follow cognition, daily function and tolerability.','Agent selection depends on comorbidity, adherence and formulation.'], [['Cholinesterase inhibitor','Symptomatic AD option'],['Expectation','Modest variable benefit'],['Monitoring','Function and adverse effects']], [NG], 'clinical_scenario')
q('A patient develops syncope and marked bradycardia after starting donepezil while taking another rate-slowing drug. What is the most appropriate response?', 'Assess urgently, review the cholinesterase inhibitor and interacting medicines, and evaluate conduction disease',[
 ('Increase donepezil because syncope proves cognitive benefit','Bradycardia and syncope can be important adverse effects.'),('Ignore pulse because the drug acts only in the brain','Cholinergic effects can influence cardiac conduction.'),('Add a second cholinesterase inhibitor immediately','Combining these agents is not a remedy for toxicity.')],
 'A cognitive drug still has systemic effects', '**Vagotonic effects can contribute to bradycardia or heart block.**', ['Other rate-slowing drugs can increase concern.','Evaluate injury and additional causes of syncope.'], '**Reassess the treatment balance.**', ['Dose interruption or modification may be required.','Record the event and plan appropriate monitoring.'], [['Symptom','Syncope'],['Mechanism','Potential cholinergic bradycardia'],['Action','Urgent assessment and medication review']], [NG], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Which patient most clearly fits a usual role for memantine under NICE dementia guidance?', 'A patient with severe AD, or moderate AD when cholinesterase inhibitors are contraindicated or not tolerated',[
 ('Every cognitively normal person with a family history','This is not an established preventive indication.'),('A patient with pure frontotemporal dementia as routine treatment','NICE does not recommend it for FTD.'),('Every person with isolated subjective forgetfulness','A diagnosis and treatment indication must first be established.')],
 'Memantine has a stage-dependent symptomatic role', '**Memantine is an NMDA receptor antagonist used in selected moderate and severe AD.**', ['It may also be added to an existing cholinesterase inhibitor according to severity and guidance.','Review kidney function for dose selection.'], '**Do not equate symptomatic treatment with disease eradication.**', ['Monitor function and adverse effects.','Drug treatment complements support and care planning.'], [['Mechanism','NMDA antagonism'],['Role','Selected moderate/severe AD'],['Renal impairment','Dose review']], [NG])
q('A person has well-characterized behavioral variant FTD without evidence of coexisting AD. Which statement about AD cognitive medicines is most appropriate?', 'Do not routinely offer cholinesterase inhibitors or memantine solely for FTD',[
 ('Donepezil reliably reverses the frontal behavior syndrome','This benefit has not been established.'),('Memantine is required because all dementias share the same drug response','Dementia subtype affects treatment selection.'),('No supportive or behavioral treatment is possible','Environmental, caregiver and symptom-focused interventions remain important.')],
 'Treatment should follow the underlying syndrome', '**An AD symptomatic drug should not be generalized to all neurodegeneration.**', ['FTD often requires behavioral and communication strategies.','Look for treatable mood, sleep and safety problems.'], '**Support the patient and household.**', ['Assess financial vulnerability, driving and impulsivity.','Consider genetic counseling in the appropriate setting.'], [['AD drugs','Not routine FTD treatment'],['Behavior','Individualized interventions'],['Support','Central to care']], [NG,FT])
q('For pure vascular dementia without suspected AD or Lewy body disease, what is the best statement about cholinesterase inhibitors under NICE guidance?', 'They are not routinely offered solely for the vascular dementia diagnosis',[
 ('They replace all vascular risk treatment','Risk management remains central.'),('They must be given after every silent MRI infarct','Imaging findings alone do not create an indication.'),('They can never be considered if vascular disease is present','Coexisting AD or Lewy body pathology can provide a separate rationale.')],
 'Mixed disease changes a treatment decision', '**NICE restricts consideration of these cognitive drugs in vascular dementia to suspected relevant comorbid neurodegenerative disease.**', ['Assess the clinical pattern rather than MRI labels alone.','Prevent further vascular injury according to the individual indications.'], '**Keep functional goals explicit.**', ['Rehabilitation and practical support can be valuable.','Review medicines and vascular risks systematically.'], [['Pure vascular disease','No routine AD-drug indication'],['Mixed AD/Lewy disease','May alter choice'],['Prevention','Address vascular risks']], [NG])
q('Which patient best matches the US labeled clinical stage for initiating lecanemab?', 'A patient with AD-related MCI or mild dementia and confirmed amyloid pathology after safety assessment',[
 ('A patient with severe dementia regardless of amyloid status','This is outside the population in which treatment was initiated in trials and the labeled initiation stage.'),('An asymptomatic person selected by APOE genotype alone','Genotype alone does not establish the labeled treatment population.'),('A patient with a positive cognitive screen but no etiological evaluation','Amyloid confirmation and a clinical safety assessment are required.')],
 'Eligibility involves stage, biology and safety', '**Lecanemab targets amyloid and is initiated in early symptomatic AD under the US label.**', ['Discuss the magnitude and uncertainty of individual benefit.','Assess MRI findings, comorbidity, medicines and ability to complete monitoring.'], '**Confirm applicable local approval and access rules.**', ['US labeling is not a universal worldwide authorization.','Treatment slows decline in studied populations rather than restoring normal cognition.'], [['Stage','MCI or mild AD dementia'],['Biology','Amyloid confirmation'],['Safety','MRI and individualized review']], [LEC], 'clinical_scenario')
q('An amyloid-antibody recipient develops new headache, confusion and a focal deficit. What complication must be urgently considered?', 'Amyloid-related imaging abnormalities, including edema or hemorrhagic changes',[
 ('A harmless expected infusion effect requiring no assessment','Focal deficits and confusion require prompt evaluation.'),('Proof of rapid neuronal regeneration','This is not a recognized interpretation.'),('A contraindication to any brain imaging','MRI is central to ARIA assessment.')],
 'Recognize symptomatic ARIA promptly', '**ARIA-E involves edema/effusion and ARIA-H involves hemorrhagic findings.**', ['Symptoms can overlap with ischemic stroke.','Severe or fatal events have occurred.'], '**Arrange urgent clinical assessment and imaging.**', ['Follow the product-specific interruption and monitoring guidance.','Inform acute stroke teams about current anti-amyloid exposure before thrombolytic decisions.'], [['ARIA-E','Edema/effusion'],['ARIA-H','Microhemorrhage/siderosis'],['New symptoms','Urgent assessment']], [LEC,DON], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Why is APOE epsilon-4 genotype discussed before initiating an amyloid-targeting antibody?', 'Homozygotes have a higher risk of ARIA, affecting informed risk counseling',[
 ('It guarantees a complete treatment response','Genotype does not guarantee benefit.'),('It alone proves symptomatic AD','Genotype is a risk factor, not a stand-alone etiological diagnosis.'),('It replaces baseline and follow-up MRI','Imaging monitoring remains necessary.')],
 'Genetic risk information changes counseling', '**APOE epsilon-4 homozygosity increases ARIA risk with these treatments.**', ['Discuss implications before testing.','Eligibility details differ across regulatory jurisdictions.'], '**Use the result within the full risk assessment.**', ['Consider baseline hemorrhagic markers and anticoagulant exposure.','Do not reduce safety monitoring because a patient is a noncarrier.'], [['APOE genotype','ARIA risk information'],['Amyloid test','Disease pathology evidence'],['MRI','Independent safety requirement']], [LEC,DON])
q('Under the July 2026 US lecanemab label, which MRI surveillance strategy is recommended?', 'Recent baseline MRI and MRI after 1, 2, 3 and 6 months of treatment, with additional assessment for suggestive symptoms',[
 ('A baseline CT alone followed by no surveillance','MRI monitoring is required for ARIA assessment.'),('One MRI after the entire treatment course','This misses early treatment risks.'),('A schedule copied from another antibody without checking the current label','Schedules are product-specific and have changed.')],
 'Monitoring must follow the current prescribing information', '**ARIA often occurs early, so surveillance is proactive rather than symptom-only.**', ['The July 2026 US label specifies MRI after 1, 2, 3 and 6 months.','Older infusion-number schedules should not be substituted without checking current guidance.'], '**Recheck the product label at treatment initiation.**', ['New neurological symptoms warrant prompt review even after a recent scan.','Document an emergency contact and escalation plan.'], [['Baseline MRI','Eligibility and reference'],['Scheduled MRI','Detect asymptomatic ARIA'],['New symptoms','Additional urgent assessment']], [LEC])
q('A patient on anticoagulation asks whether anti-amyloid therapy is automatically safe if the baseline MRI has no edema. What is the best response?', 'Intracerebral hemorrhage risk and the need for anticoagulation require specialist individualized assessment',[
 ('Absence of edema removes every hemorrhagic risk','ARIA-H, CAA-related markers and concurrent drugs still matter.'),('Stop necessary anticoagulation without considering its indication','Stroke or thromboembolic risk must also be assessed.'),('Routine thrombolysis is risk-free during antibody treatment','Serious hemorrhagic risk and ARIA mimics require particular caution.')],
 'Competing vascular risks cannot be reduced to one scan', '**Anticoagulants and hemorrhage-prone imaging features can materially affect treatment decisions.**', ['A normal edema assessment is not the whole safety evaluation.','Apply the current product label and specialist protocol.'], '**Make a documented shared decision.**', ['Consider the expected benefit and the alternative risks.','Ensure emergency teams can identify antibody exposure.'], [['Anticoagulation','Important bleeding-risk context'],['MRI','Several safety markers'],['Decision','Individualized specialist review']], [LEC,DON], 'clinical_scenario')
q('A newly available plasma amyloid/tau test is proposed for screening every healthy adult. Which statement best reflects the FDA-cleared Lumipulse test use?', 'It assists evaluation of symptomatic adults aged 55 or older and is not a stand-alone population screening test',[
 ('Its clearance authorizes indiscriminate screening of all ages','That exceeds the intended population.'),('It removes the need to interpret symptoms and other findings','Clinical evaluation remains essential.'),('Every positive result establishes severe dementia','Pathology detection and functional severity differ.')],
 'A test clearance has a defined intended use', '**The plasma ratio supports assessment in a specified symptomatic population.**', ['A positive result must be interpreted within pretest probability and the full evaluation.','Indeterminate results and assay limitations require a plan.'], '**Do not generalize one assay’s performance to every commercial biomarker.**', ['Confirm the exact validated test and intended use.','Discuss how results will change care before ordering.'], [['Population','Symptomatic age 55 or older'],['Role','Diagnostic aid'],['Limit','Not stand-alone population screening']], [BLOOD])
q('Rapidly progressive cognitive decline over eight weeks is accompanied by myoclonus and ataxia. Which investigation is particularly useful when sporadic CJD is suspected?', 'CSF RT-QuIC interpreted with clinical assessment and brain MRI',[
 ('A positive nonspecific CSF 14-3-3 result as absolute proof','14-3-3 reflects neuronal injury and is not specific proof.'),('Amyloid PET as the sole prion diagnostic test','This evaluates another pathological target.'),('A normal routine blood count to exclude CJD','Routine blood counts do not exclude prion disease.')],
 'Rapid progression requires a broad urgent differential', '**RT-QuIC detects prion seeding activity and supports probable CJD in the appropriate setting.**', ['MRI can show cortical ribboning or caudate/putamen diffusion abnormalities.','Autoimmune, infectious, toxic and metabolic mimics must be assessed.'], '**Do not equate suspicion with definitive tissue diagnosis.**', ['Apply formal criteria and specialist pathways.','Follow appropriate instrument and tissue precautions when procedures are considered.'], [['RT-QuIC','Prion seeding assay'],['DWI MRI','Characteristic regional abnormalities'],['14-3-3','Nonspecific injury marker']], [PRION], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Which statement about an apparent rapidly progressive dementia is most appropriate?', 'Urgently investigate treatable inflammatory, infectious, metabolic, toxic and epileptic causes before attributing it to an irreversible degeneration',[
 ('Myoclonus makes every case CJD','Myoclonus has several causes.'),('An eight-week course is typical enough for routine annual review','Rapid progression needs expedited assessment.'),('A negative initial screen excludes all autoimmune and seizure-related disorders','Further targeted testing may be needed when suspicion remains.')],
 'Tempo determines urgency', '**Rapid cognitive deterioration has potentially reversible causes.**', ['Seizures, encephalitis, drug toxicity and systemic disorders can mimic degeneration.','MRI, EEG and CSF testing are chosen according to the clinical pattern.'], '**Stabilize acute complications while investigating.**', ['Address seizures, nutrition and safety.','Reassess the working diagnosis as results arrive.'], [['Weeks to months','Urgent evaluation'],['Mimics','Often treatment-relevant'],['Tests','Clinical pattern directs selection']], [EVAL,PRION])
q('A person repeatedly asks the same questions for four hours but knows their identity, has no focal deficit and completely recovers new learning within 24 hours. Which syndrome is most consistent after acute alternatives are excluded?', 'Transient global amnesia',[
 ('Established dementia from one isolated episode','Dementia requires a persistent decline rather than a fully resolving isolated attack.'),('Transient epileptic amnesia in every case','Brief recurrent attacks and epileptic features would raise that alternative.'),('Delirium solely because memory is impaired','TGA predominantly affects memory without the broader attention/awareness syndrome.')],
 'Isolated transient amnesia has a distinct differential', '**TGA causes abrupt anterograde amnesia with preserved personal identity.**', ['Recovery occurs within 24 hours by definition.','A gap for the episode can remain.'], '**Atypical features require urgent investigation.**', ['Persistent deficits, reduced consciousness, focal signs or seizures argue for alternatives.','Short recurrent episodes should prompt consideration of epilepsy.'], [['TGA','Hours, resolves within 24 hours'],['Epileptic amnesia','Often brief and recurrent'],['Persistent/focal signs','Investigate alternative causes']], [TGA], 'clinical_scenario')
q('A person with dementia becomes agitated during evening care. What is the best initial management principle?', 'Assess pain, unmet needs, delirium, environment and the care interaction before selecting medication',[
 ('Use an antipsychotic for every episode immediately','Medication is not the first response to all distress.'),('Assume the behavior has no identifiable trigger','Pain, fear and communication difficulty are common contributors.'),('Use long-term physical restraint as routine prevention','This creates harm and does not address the cause.')],
 'Distress is a clinical signal', '**A structured assessment can identify modifiable drivers.**', ['Review infection, constipation, urinary retention and sleep.','Adapt communication and offer personalized activities.'], '**Use medication only for a defined indication.**', ['Severe distress or risk of harm may justify a carefully assessed antipsychotic trial.','Reassess benefit, adverse effects and continuing need.'], [['First step','Find contributors'],['Environment','Reduce triggers'],['Medication','Defined target and review']], [NG], 'clinical_scenario')
q('An antipsychotic is considered for severe dementia-related psychosis causing risk of harm despite other measures. Which prescribing principle is most appropriate?', 'Discuss mortality and cerebrovascular risks, use the lowest effective dose for the shortest appropriate duration and review regularly',[
 ('Continue indefinitely without review if the first dose sedates','Sedation is not sufficient evidence of meaningful benefit.'),('Assume all dementia subtypes tolerate dopamine blockade equally','Lewy body disease requires particular caution.'),('Prescribe solely to reduce staff workload','Treatment needs a patient-centered clinical indication.')],
 'Potential benefit must outweigh substantial harm', '**Antipsychotics in dementia carry important adverse-event risks.**', ['Document the target symptom and consent discussion.','Review whether the intervention improves distress or safety.'], '**Plan discontinuation or reduction when appropriate.**', ['NICE recommends reassessing at least every six weeks.','Continue nonpharmacological support during treatment.'], [['Indication','Severe distress or harm risk'],['Exposure','Lowest effective, shortest appropriate'],['Review','Benefit and continuing need']], [NG])
q('A new dementia diagnosis is made in a person still participating in daily decisions. Which approach to decision-making capacity is correct?', 'Assess capacity for the specific decision at the relevant time and support the person’s participation',[
 ('The diagnosis automatically removes capacity for every choice','Capacity is decision-specific, not eliminated by the diagnostic label alone.'),('A cognitive screening cutoff is the sole legal test','Clinical and legal standards require more than a screening score.'),('Defer all planning until severe impairment develops','Early preferences and support planning are valuable.')],
 'Cognitive diagnosis and decisional ability are different assessments', '**Capacity depends on the particular decision and applicable local law.**', ['Optimize communication, hearing, language and timing.','Include the person’s values and choices to the greatest extent possible.'], '**Discuss future care while participation is possible.**', ['Address driving, medication management and financial vulnerability as appropriate.','Offer caregiver support without automatically transferring every decision.'], [['Diagnosis','Clinical syndrome'],['Capacity','Task- and time-specific'],['Planning','Early supported discussion']], [NG], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Which intervention belongs in a comprehensive dementia care plan alongside medicines?', 'Personalized activity, cognitive and functional support, caregiver education, safety review and advance care discussions',[
 ('Cognitive drugs alone with no functional review','Medicines address only part of the burden.'),('Routine isolation to prevent every possible error','Unnecessary isolation can worsen well-being and function.'),('Caregiver support only after institutionalization','Caregivers need assessment and support throughout the course.')],
 'Care follows changing function and goals', '**Practical support is part of active treatment.**', ['Review nutrition, sensory impairment, mobility, sleep and daily routines.','Offer appropriate cognitive stimulation or rehabilitation interventions.'], '**Reassess patient and caregiver needs over time.**', ['Identify caregiver strain and safeguarding concerns.','Discuss preferences and escalation decisions with supported participation.'], [['Patient','Function and meaningful activity'],['Caregiver','Education and support'],['Future','Goals and advance planning']], [NG])

